IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `cla-core`

-- Civil Liability Act 2002 (NSW) -- the defences and limits on liability a claimant for personal injury meets: Part 1A
-- (ss 5H, 5I obvious and inherent risks; ss 5J-5L dangerous recreational activities), Part 3 (mental harm, ss 30, 31,
-- 33), Part 6 (intoxication, s 50) and Part 7 Division 1 (self-defence and recovery by criminals, ss 52-54A). Each is
-- gated by s 3B through `cla-core`.
--
-- SEAT: the injured person (for Part 3, the person who suffered the mental harm). Every exported question asks whether a
-- provision stops or cuts down that person's claim. The defendant and the court get no seat.
--
-- Out of this module: ss 5B-5E (the general negligence and causation tests: judgments left wholly to the court), s 5M
-- (risk warnings), s 5N (contract waivers), ss 5O, 5P (professionals), s 5Q, ss 5R-5T (contributory negligence: the
-- court's percentage), s 32 (duty of care for mental harm: a foreseeability judgment), s 49, Part 7 Division 2; see
-- NOTES.md.
--
-- Based on content from the New South Wales Legislation website (as collected by the Open Australian Legal Corpus on
-- 10 March 2025, corpus version dated 16 June 2022; unofficial, possibly out of date). CC BY 4.0. BASELINE ENCODING. Not
-- for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Civil Liability Act 2002 -- defences and limits on liability`

§§ `The nouns`

-- s 5K "recreational activity includes (a) any sport ..., (b) any pursuit or activity engaged in for enjoyment,
-- relaxation or leisure, and (c) any pursuit or activity engaged in at a place (such as a beach, park or other public
-- open space) where people ordinarily engage in sport or in any pursuit or activity for enjoyment, relaxation or leisure."
DECLARE `What I was doing` IS ONE OF
    `a sport, organised or not`
    `something done for enjoyment, relaxation or leisure`
    `something done at a place where people ordinarily play sport or relax, such as a beach or park`
    `none of these activities`

-- s 30(5) "close member of the family of a victim", from the claimant's side.
DECLARE `Who I am to the victim` IS ONE OF
    `the victim's parent, or a person with parental responsibility for the victim`
    `the victim's spouse or partner (the last one, if more than one would qualify)`
    `the victim's child or stepchild, or a person the victim has parental responsibility for`
    `the victim's brother, sister, half-brother, half-sister, stepbrother or stepsister`
    `another relative, a friend, or no relation`

-- s 54, s 54A: the claimant's own conduct.
DECLARE `My conduct` IS ONE OF
    `an offence`
    `not an offence only because of my mental health impairment or cognitive impairment at the time`
    `not an offence`

DECLARE `What section 50 does to my claim` IS ONE OF
    `section 50 does not apply to my claim`
    `no damages: the court is not satisfied the injury would likely have happened even if I had not been intoxicated`
    `my damages are reduced by at least 25% for presumed contributory negligence`
    `section 50 does not reduce my damages: the court is satisfied my intoxication did not contribute`
    `the Act does not settle what section 50 does yet`

DECLARE `What self-defence means for my claim` IS ONE OF
    `the defendant is not liable to me: self-defence under section 52`
    `no damages unless the court finds the case exceptional and refusing damages harsh and unjust, and then none for non-economic loss (section 53)`
    `sections 52 and 53 do not protect the defendant on these facts`
    `the Act does not settle the self-defence question yet`

DECLARE `What my own offence means for my claim` IS ONE OF
    `no damages: section 54 bars my claim`
    `no damages for non-economic loss or for lost earnings (section 54A)`
    `sections 54 and 54A do not limit my damages`
    `the Act does not settle the offence question`

-- The purposes listed in s 52(2).
DECLARE `What the defendant was doing it for` IS ONE OF
    `to defend themselves or another person`
    `to prevent or end the unlawful deprivation of their own or another person's liberty`
    `to protect property from unlawful taking, destruction, damage or interference`
    `to prevent criminal trespass on land or premises, or remove a trespasser`
    `none of these purposes`

§§ `Part 1A Division 4 -- obvious and inherent risks`

-- s 5H(1) "A person (the defendant) does not owe a duty of care to another person (the plaintiff) to warn of an obvious
-- risk to the plaintiff." (2) "This section does not apply if (a) the plaintiff has requested advice or information
-- about the risk from the defendant, or (b) the defendant is required by a written law to warn ..., or (c) the defendant
-- is a professional and the risk is a risk of the death of or personal injury to the plaintiff from the provision of a
-- professional service". s 5F(1) obvious risk: obvious "to a reasonable person in the position of that person" (asked
-- as a fact). JUST TRUE = s 5H removes the duty to warn; JUST FALSE = s 5H does not, and (3) "does not give rise to a
-- presumption of a duty to warn".
@export Does section 5H mean the defendant owed me no duty to warn me of the risk (ss 5F, 5H)?
GIVEN `my kind of claim` IS A `Kind of claim` @desc What your claim is. One of: "negligence, or another claim for death or personal injury not listed here"; "an intentional act the defendant did meaning to injure or kill"; "sexual assault or other sexual misconduct by the defendant"; "vicarious liability for someone else's intentional act or sexual misconduct" (for example a claim against an employer for an employee's abuse); "a dust disease claim of the kind brought in the Dust Diseases Tribunal"; "injury or death from smoking or other use of tobacco products"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2017"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accidents Compensation Act 1999 or the Motor Accidents Act 1988"; "work injury damages against an employer under Division 3 of Part 5 of the Workers Compensation Act 1987" (not a child abuse claim, which that Division does not cover); "statutory compensation or a statutory benefit" (workers compensation, dust diseases compensation, anti-discrimination compensation, a sporting injuries benefit, or victims support for economic loss)
      `the risk was obvious` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the risk would have been obvious to a reasonable person in your position (including a risk that is patent or a matter of common knowledge, even if unlikely or not physically observable)
      `I asked the defendant about the risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you had asked the defendant for advice or information about the risk
      `a written law required a warning` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a written law required the defendant to warn you of the risk
      `it was a professional service risk of injury or death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant is a professional and the risk was of your death or personal injury from a professional service the defendant provided
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does section 5H remove the duty to warn me` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 3B answer for` `my kind of claim` `and` `Part 1A Divisions 1 to 4 and 8: negligence, causation, obvious and inherent risks, contributory negligence`
    WHEN JUST g THEN
        IF     g
           AND `the risk was obvious`
           AND (NOT `I asked the defendant about the risk`)
           AND (NOT `a written law required a warning`)
           AND (NOT `it was a professional service risk of injury or death`)
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE JUST FALSE
    OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (fork F1)

-- s 5I(1) "A person is not liable in negligence for harm suffered by another person as a result of the materialisation
-- of an inherent risk." (2) "An inherent risk is a risk of something occurring that cannot be avoided by the exercise of
-- reasonable care and skill." (3) "This section does not operate to exclude liability in connection with a duty to warn
-- of a risk."
@export Does section 5I stop my claim because the harm came from an inherent risk (s 5I)?
GIVEN `my kind of claim` IS A `Kind of claim` @desc What your claim is. One of: "negligence, or another claim for death or personal injury not listed here"; "an intentional act the defendant did meaning to injure or kill"; "sexual assault or other sexual misconduct by the defendant"; "vicarious liability for someone else's intentional act or sexual misconduct" (for example a claim against an employer for an employee's abuse); "a dust disease claim of the kind brought in the Dust Diseases Tribunal"; "injury or death from smoking or other use of tobacco products"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2017"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accidents Compensation Act 1999 or the Motor Accidents Act 1988"; "work injury damages against an employer under Division 3 of Part 5 of the Workers Compensation Act 1987" (not a child abuse claim, which that Division does not cover); "statutory compensation or a statutory benefit" (workers compensation, dust diseases compensation, anti-discrimination compensation, a sporting injuries benefit, or victims support for economic loss)
      `the risk could not have been avoided by reasonable care` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the harm came from a risk that could not have been avoided by the exercise of reasonable care and skill
      `my claim is about a failure to warn me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is that the defendant failed to warn you of the risk
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does section 5I stop my claim` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 3B answer for` `my kind of claim` `and` `Part 1A Divisions 1 to 4 and 8: negligence, causation, obvious and inherent risks, contributory negligence`
    WHEN JUST g THEN
        IF g AND `the risk could not have been avoided by reasonable care` AND (NOT `my claim is about a failure to warn me`)
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE JUST FALSE
    OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (fork F1)

§§ `Part 1A Division 5 -- dangerous recreational activities`

-- s 5J(1) "This Division applies only in respect of liability in negligence for harm to a person (the plaintiff)
-- resulting from a recreational activity engaged in by the plaintiff." s 5K "dangerous recreational activity means a
-- recreational activity that involves a significant risk of physical harm." s 5L(1) "not liable in negligence for harm
-- suffered by another person (the plaintiff) as a result of the materialisation of an obvious risk of a dangerous
-- recreational activity engaged in by the plaintiff." (2) "whether or not the plaintiff was aware of the risk". The
-- "includes" list in s 5K is open: an activity on none of its limbs answers NOTHING.
@export Does section 5L stop my claim because I was hurt by an obvious risk of a dangerous recreational activity (ss 5J, 5K, 5L)?
GIVEN `my kind of claim` IS A `Kind of claim` @desc What your claim is. One of: "negligence, or another claim for death or personal injury not listed here"; "an intentional act the defendant did meaning to injure or kill"; "sexual assault or other sexual misconduct by the defendant"; "vicarious liability for someone else's intentional act or sexual misconduct" (for example a claim against an employer for an employee's abuse); "a dust disease claim of the kind brought in the Dust Diseases Tribunal"; "injury or death from smoking or other use of tobacco products"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2017"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accidents Compensation Act 1999 or the Motor Accidents Act 1988"; "work injury damages against an employer under Division 3 of Part 5 of the Workers Compensation Act 1987" (not a child abuse claim, which that Division does not cover); "statutory compensation or a statutory benefit" (workers compensation, dust diseases compensation, anti-discrimination compensation, a sporting injuries benefit, or victims support for economic loss)
      `I was taking part in the activity myself` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were yourself engaged in the activity when you were harmed (not a spectator or bystander)
      `what I was doing` IS A `What I was doing` @desc The activity. One of: "a sport, organised or not"; "something done for enjoyment, relaxation or leisure"; "something done at a place where people ordinarily play sport or relax, such as a beach or park"; "none of these activities"
      `it involved a significant risk of physical harm` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the activity involved a significant risk of physical harm
      `the harm came from an obvious risk of it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the harm came from a risk of the activity that would have been obvious to a reasonable person in your position (whether or not you were aware of it)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does section 5L stop my claim` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 3B answer for` `my kind of claim` `and` `Part 1A Divisions 5 to 7: recreational activities, professional negligence, non-delegable duties`
    WHEN JUST g THEN
        BRANCH IF (NOT g) THEN JUST FALSE
               IF (NOT `I was taking part in the activity myself`) THEN JUST FALSE
               IF (NOT `it involved a significant risk of physical harm`) THEN JUST FALSE
               IF (NOT `the harm came from an obvious risk of it`) THEN JUST FALSE
               IF `what I was doing` EQUALS `none of these activities` THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (the list is open: s 5K "includes")
               OTHERWISE JUST TRUE
    OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (fork F1)

§§ `Part 3 -- mental harm`

-- s 30(5): "close member of the family of a victim means" (a) a parent or person with parental responsibility, (b) the
-- spouse or partner, (c) a child or stepchild or person for whom the victim has parental responsibility, (d) a sibling,
-- half-sibling or step-sibling. A "means" list: closed.
GIVEN `the relation` IS A `Who I am to the victim`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `I am a close member of the victim's family as` `the relation` IF
    (NOT (`the relation` EQUALS `another relative, a friend, or no relation`))

-- s 30(1) applies to pure mental harm "arising wholly or partly from mental or nervous shock in connection with another
-- person (the victim) being killed, injured or put in peril by the act or omission of the defendant". (2) "The plaintiff
-- is not entitled to recover damages for pure mental harm unless (a) the plaintiff witnessed, at the scene, the victim
-- being killed, injured or put in peril, or (b) the plaintiff is a close member of the family of the victim." (4) "No
-- damages ... if the recovery of damages from the defendant by or through the victim ... would be prevented by any
-- provision of this Act or any other written or unwritten law." s 31 "There is no liability to pay damages for pure
-- mental harm resulting from negligence unless the harm consists of a recognised psychiatric illness." s 28(1): Part 3
-- (except s 29) applies to claims for mental harm resulting from negligence. JUST TRUE = barred.
@export Do sections 30 and 31 stop my claim for pure mental harm (for example shock at seeing someone killed or injured, or learning a close family member was)?
GIVEN `my kind of claim` IS A `Kind of claim` @desc What your claim is. One of: "negligence, or another claim for death or personal injury not listed here"; "an intentional act the defendant did meaning to injure or kill"; "sexual assault or other sexual misconduct by the defendant"; "vicarious liability for someone else's intentional act or sexual misconduct" (for example a claim against an employer for an employee's abuse); "a dust disease claim of the kind brought in the Dust Diseases Tribunal"; "injury or death from smoking or other use of tobacco products"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2017"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accidents Compensation Act 1999 or the Motor Accidents Act 1988"; "work injury damages against an employer under Division 3 of Part 5 of the Workers Compensation Act 1987" (not a child abuse claim, which that Division does not cover); "statutory compensation or a statutory benefit" (workers compensation, dust diseases compensation, anti-discrimination compensation, a sporting injuries benefit, or victims support for economic loss)
      `my mental harm results from negligence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is for mental harm resulting from negligence (a failure to take reasonable care)
      `I had no physical injury of my own that led to it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your mental harm is pure mental harm: not a consequence of a physical (or other) personal injury of your own
      `it came from shock at someone else being killed, injured or put in peril by the defendant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your mental harm arose wholly or partly from mental or nervous shock in connection with another person (the victim) being killed, injured or put in peril by the defendant
      `I witnessed it at the scene` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you witnessed, at the scene, the victim being killed, injured or put in peril
      `who I am to the victim` IS A `Who I am to the victim` @desc Who you are to the victim. One of: "the victim's parent, or a person with parental responsibility for the victim"; "the victim's spouse or partner (the last one, if more than one would qualify)"; "the victim's child or stepchild, or a person the victim has parental responsibility for"; "the victim's brother, sister, half-brother, half-sister, stepbrother or stepsister"; "another relative, a friend, or no relation"
      `the victim could not recover from the defendant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the victim (or their estate or family) would be prevented by law from recovering damages from the defendant for the act or omission
      `it is a recognised psychiatric illness` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your mental harm is a recognised psychiatric illness (diagnosed, for example post-traumatic stress disorder or a major depressive disorder)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Do sections 30 and 31 stop my claim for pure mental harm` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 3B answer for` `my kind of claim` `and` `Part 3: mental harm`
    WHEN JUST g THEN
        BRANCH IF (NOT g) THEN JUST FALSE
               IF (NOT `my mental harm results from negligence`) THEN JUST FALSE
               IF (NOT `I had no physical injury of my own that led to it`) THEN JUST FALSE
               IF (NOT `it is a recognised psychiatric illness`) THEN JUST TRUE
               IF (NOT `it came from shock at someone else being killed, injured or put in peril by the defendant`) THEN JUST FALSE
               IF `the victim could not recover from the defendant` THEN JUST TRUE
               IF `I witnessed it at the scene` THEN JUST FALSE
               IF `I am a close member of the victim's family as` `who I am to the victim` THEN JUST FALSE
               OTHERWISE JUST TRUE
    OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (fork F1)

-- s 30(3) "Any damages to be awarded to the plaintiff for pure mental harm are to be reduced in the same proportion as any
-- reduction in the damages that may be recovered from the defendant by or through the victim on the basis of the
-- contributory negligence of the victim."
@export How much are my damages for pure mental harm reduced because the victim was partly to blame (s 30(3))?
GIVEN `my kind of claim` IS A `Kind of claim` @desc What your claim is. One of: "negligence, or another claim for death or personal injury not listed here"; "an intentional act the defendant did meaning to injure or kill"; "sexual assault or other sexual misconduct by the defendant"; "vicarious liability for someone else's intentional act or sexual misconduct" (for example a claim against an employer for an employee's abuse); "a dust disease claim of the kind brought in the Dust Diseases Tribunal"; "injury or death from smoking or other use of tobacco products"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2017"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accidents Compensation Act 1999 or the Motor Accidents Act 1988"; "work injury damages against an employer under Division 3 of Part 5 of the Workers Compensation Act 1987" (not a child abuse claim, which that Division does not cover); "statutory compensation or a statutory benefit" (workers compensation, dust diseases compensation, anti-discrimination compensation, a sporting injuries benefit, or victims support for economic loss)
      `my damages before the reduction` IS A NUMBER @desc Your damages for pure mental harm before any reduction, in dollars
      `the victim's contributory negligence reduction` IS A NUMBER @desc The percentage by which the victim's own damages are (or would be) reduced for the victim's contributory negligence, as a number from 0 to 100 (for example 30 for 30%)
GIVETH AN `Amount under the section`
`My damages for pure mental harm after the victim's reduction` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 3B answer for` `my kind of claim` `and` `Part 3: mental harm`
    WHEN JUST g THEN
        IF g
        THEN `the amount is` (`my damages before the reduction` TIMES ((100 MINUS `the victim's contributory negligence reduction`) DIVIDED BY 100))
        ELSE `this section does not apply to my claim`
    OTHERWISE `the Act does not settle the amount` -- NOTHING-kind (fork F1)

-- s 33 "A court cannot make an award of damages for economic loss for consequential mental harm resulting from negligence
-- unless the harm consists of a recognised psychiatric illness."
@export Does section 33 stop me getting damages for lost income or other economic loss caused by mental harm that followed my own injury?
GIVEN `my kind of claim` IS A `Kind of claim` @desc What your claim is. One of: "negligence, or another claim for death or personal injury not listed here"; "an intentional act the defendant did meaning to injure or kill"; "sexual assault or other sexual misconduct by the defendant"; "vicarious liability for someone else's intentional act or sexual misconduct" (for example a claim against an employer for an employee's abuse); "a dust disease claim of the kind brought in the Dust Diseases Tribunal"; "injury or death from smoking or other use of tobacco products"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2017"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accidents Compensation Act 1999 or the Motor Accidents Act 1988"; "work injury damages against an employer under Division 3 of Part 5 of the Workers Compensation Act 1987" (not a child abuse claim, which that Division does not cover); "statutory compensation or a statutory benefit" (workers compensation, dust diseases compensation, anti-discrimination compensation, a sporting injuries benefit, or victims support for economic loss)
      `my mental harm results from negligence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your mental harm results from negligence
      `it followed a personal injury of my own` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your mental harm is a consequence of a personal injury of your own (consequential mental harm)
      `it is a recognised psychiatric illness` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the mental harm is a recognised psychiatric illness
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does section 33 stop my economic loss claim for consequential mental harm` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 3B answer for` `my kind of claim` `and` `Part 3: mental harm`
    WHEN JUST g THEN
        IF g AND `my mental harm results from negligence` AND `it followed a personal injury of my own` AND (NOT `it is a recognised psychiatric illness`)
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE JUST FALSE
    OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (fork F1)

§§ `Part 6 -- intoxication`

-- s 50(1) applies "when it is established that the person whose death, injury or damage is the subject of proceedings
-- ... was at the time ... intoxicated to the extent that the person's capacity to exercise reasonable care and skill was
-- impaired." (2) "A court is not to award damages ... unless satisfied that the death, injury or damage ... is likely to
-- have occurred even if the person had not been intoxicated." (3) if so satisfied, "it is to be presumed that the person
-- was contributorily negligent unless the court is satisfied that the person's intoxication did not contribute in any
-- way". (4) the reduction is "25% or a greater percentage". (5) "This section does not apply in a case where the court is
-- satisfied that the intoxication was not self-induced." s 48: intoxicated = "under the influence of alcohol or a drug
-- (whether or not taken for a medicinal purpose and whether or not lawfully taken)".
@export What does section 50 do to my claim, given I was intoxicated when I was injured (ss 47, 48, 50)?
GIVEN `my kind of claim` IS A `Kind of claim` @desc What your claim is. One of: "negligence, or another claim for death or personal injury not listed here"; "an intentional act the defendant did meaning to injure or kill"; "sexual assault or other sexual misconduct by the defendant"; "vicarious liability for someone else's intentional act or sexual misconduct" (for example a claim against an employer for an employee's abuse); "a dust disease claim of the kind brought in the Dust Diseases Tribunal"; "injury or death from smoking or other use of tobacco products"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2017"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accidents Compensation Act 1999 or the Motor Accidents Act 1988"; "work injury damages against an employer under Division 3 of Part 5 of the Workers Compensation Act 1987" (not a child abuse claim, which that Division does not cover); "statutory compensation or a statutory benefit" (workers compensation, dust diseases compensation, anti-discrimination compensation, a sporting injuries benefit, or victims support for economic loss)
      `I was intoxicated and it impaired my capacity to take care` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, at the time of the act or omission that caused your injury, you were under the influence of alcohol or a drug (prescribed or not, lawful or not) to the extent that your capacity to exercise reasonable care and skill was impaired
      `the court's finding that it was not self-induced` IS A `The court's finding` @desc Whether the court is satisfied that your intoxication was not self-induced (for example your drink was spiked). One of: "the court has found that it is so", "the court has found that it is not so", "the court has not decided"
      `the court's finding that it would likely have happened anyway` IS A `The court's finding` @desc Whether the court is satisfied that the injury (or some other injury) is likely to have happened even if you had not been intoxicated. One of: "the court has found that it is so", "the court has found that it is not so", "the court has not decided"
      `the court's finding that my intoxication did not contribute` IS A `The court's finding` @desc Whether the court is satisfied that your intoxication did not contribute in any way to the cause of the injury. One of: "the court has found that it is so", "the court has found that it is not so", "the court has not decided"
GIVETH A `What section 50 does to my claim`
`What does section 50 do to my claim` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 3B answer for` `my kind of claim` `and` `the rest of Part 6: no recovery when intoxicated`
    WHEN JUST g THEN
        BRANCH IF (NOT g) THEN `section 50 does not apply to my claim`
               IF (NOT `I was intoxicated and it impaired my capacity to take care`) THEN `section 50 does not apply to my claim`
               IF `the court's finding that it was not self-induced` EQUALS `the court has found that it is so` THEN `section 50 does not apply to my claim`
               IF `the court's finding that it was not self-induced` EQUALS `the court has not decided` THEN `the Act does not settle what section 50 does yet`
               IF `the court's finding that it would likely have happened anyway` EQUALS `the court has found that it is not so` THEN `no damages: the court is not satisfied the injury would likely have happened even if I had not been intoxicated`
               IF `the court's finding that it would likely have happened anyway` EQUALS `the court has not decided` THEN `the Act does not settle what section 50 does yet`
               IF `the court's finding that my intoxication did not contribute` EQUALS `the court has found that it is so` THEN `section 50 does not reduce my damages: the court is satisfied my intoxication did not contribute`
               IF `the court's finding that my intoxication did not contribute` EQUALS `the court has not decided` THEN `the Act does not settle what section 50 does yet`
               OTHERWISE `my damages are reduced by at least 25% for presumed contributory negligence`
    OTHERWISE `the Act does not settle what section 50 does yet` -- NOTHING-kind (fork F1)

§§ `Part 7 Division 1 -- self-defence and recovery by criminals`

-- s 52(1) "A person does not incur a liability ... arising from any conduct of the person carried out in self-defence,
-- but only if the conduct to which the person was responding (a) was unlawful, or (b) would have been unlawful if the
-- other person ... had not had a mental health impairment or a cognitive impairment". (2) self-defence "if and only if"
-- the person believes the conduct is necessary for a listed purpose "and the conduct is a reasonable response in the
-- circumstances as he or she perceives them". (3) not where the force "involves the intentional or reckless infliction of
-- death only (a) to protect property, or (b) to prevent criminal trespass or to remove a person committing criminal
-- trespass". s 53: if s 52 fails only because the response was not reasonable, no damages unless "the circumstances of
-- the case are exceptional" and refusal "would be harsh and unjust", and then none for non-economic loss. Whether the
-- response was reasonable is the court's finding.
@export Can the person who injured me escape liability by saying they acted in self-defence (ss 52, 53)?
GIVEN `my kind of claim` IS A `Kind of claim` @desc What your claim is. One of: "negligence, or another claim for death or personal injury not listed here"; "an intentional act the defendant did meaning to injure or kill"; "sexual assault or other sexual misconduct by the defendant"; "vicarious liability for someone else's intentional act or sexual misconduct" (for example a claim against an employer for an employee's abuse); "a dust disease claim of the kind brought in the Dust Diseases Tribunal"; "injury or death from smoking or other use of tobacco products"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2017"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accidents Compensation Act 1999 or the Motor Accidents Act 1988"; "work injury damages against an employer under Division 3 of Part 5 of the Workers Compensation Act 1987" (not a child abuse claim, which that Division does not cover); "statutory compensation or a statutory benefit" (workers compensation, dust diseases compensation, anti-discrimination compensation, a sporting injuries benefit, or victims support for economic loss)
      `what the defendant was doing it for` IS A `What the defendant was doing it for` @desc What the defendant believed their conduct was necessary for. One of: "to defend themselves or another person"; "to prevent or end the unlawful deprivation of their own or another person's liberty"; "to protect property from unlawful taking, destruction, damage or interference"; "to prevent criminal trespass on land or premises, or remove a trespasser"; "none of these purposes"
      `my conduct they responded to was unlawful` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your conduct that the defendant was responding to was unlawful, or would have been unlawful but for a mental health impairment or cognitive impairment you had at the time
      `the court's finding that it was a reasonable response` IS A `The court's finding` @desc Whether the court finds the defendant's conduct was a reasonable response in the circumstances as the defendant perceived them. One of: "the court has found that it is so", "the court has found that it is not so", "the court has not decided"
      `they intentionally or recklessly inflicted death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant's force involved the intentional or reckless infliction of death (relevant only where the defendant acted only to protect property or to deal with a trespasser)
GIVETH A `What self-defence means for my claim`
`Can the defendant rely on self-defence` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 3B answer for` `my kind of claim` `and` `Part 7: self-defence and recovery by criminals`
    WHEN JUST g THEN
        BRANCH IF (NOT g) THEN `sections 52 and 53 do not protect the defendant on these facts`
               IF `what the defendant was doing it for` EQUALS `none of these purposes` THEN `sections 52 and 53 do not protect the defendant on these facts`
               IF (NOT `my conduct they responded to was unlawful`) THEN `sections 52 and 53 do not protect the defendant on these facts`
               IF `they intentionally or recklessly inflicted death` AND `the purpose was only property or trespass` THEN `sections 52 and 53 do not protect the defendant on these facts`
               IF `the court's finding that it was a reasonable response` EQUALS `the court has found that it is so` THEN `the defendant is not liable to me: self-defence under section 52`
               IF `the court's finding that it was a reasonable response` EQUALS `the court has found that it is not so` THEN `no damages unless the court finds the case exceptional and refusing damages harsh and unjust, and then none for non-economic loss (section 53)`
               OTHERWISE `the Act does not settle the self-defence question yet`
    OTHERWISE `the Act does not settle the self-defence question yet` -- NOTHING-kind (fork F1)
    WHERE
        `the purpose was only property or trespass` MEANS
               `what the defendant was doing it for` EQUALS `to protect property from unlawful taking, destruction, damage or interference`
            OR `what the defendant was doing it for` EQUALS `to prevent criminal trespass on land or premises, or remove a trespasser`

-- s 54(1) "A court is not to award damages ... if the court is satisfied that (a) the death of, or the injury or damage
-- to, the person ... occurred at the time of, or following, conduct of that person that, on the balance of
-- probabilities, constitutes a serious offence, and (b) that conduct contributed materially to the death, injury or
-- damage or to the risk of death, injury or damage." (2) not "if the conduct of the defendant that caused the death,
-- injury or damage concerned constitutes an offence". (3) "A serious offence is an offence punishable by imprisonment
-- for 6 months or more." s 54A: the same where the conduct would have been a serious offence but for the claimant's
-- mental health or cognitive impairment; then no damages for non-economic loss or for loss of earnings; (4) not where
-- the defendant's conduct was an offence or would have been but for the defendant's impairment.
@export Does my own criminal conduct at the time stop or limit my damages (ss 54, 54A)?
GIVEN `my kind of claim` IS A `Kind of claim` @desc What your claim is. One of: "negligence, or another claim for death or personal injury not listed here"; "an intentional act the defendant did meaning to injure or kill"; "sexual assault or other sexual misconduct by the defendant"; "vicarious liability for someone else's intentional act or sexual misconduct" (for example a claim against an employer for an employee's abuse); "a dust disease claim of the kind brought in the Dust Diseases Tribunal"; "injury or death from smoking or other use of tobacco products"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2017"; "a motor accident claim under the Motor Accidents Compensation Act 1999 or the Motor Accidents Act 1988"; "work injury damages against an employer under Division 3 of Part 5 of the Workers Compensation Act 1987" (not a child abuse claim, which that Division does not cover); "statutory compensation or a statutory benefit" (workers compensation, dust diseases compensation, anti-discrimination compensation, a sporting injuries benefit, or victims support for economic loss)
      `I was hurt during or after my own conduct` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your injury happened at the time of, or following, conduct of your own that may have been an offence
      `my conduct` IS A `My conduct` @desc What that conduct was, on the balance of probabilities (whether or not you were charged or convicted). One of: "an offence"; "not an offence only because of my mental health impairment or cognitive impairment at the time"; "not an offence"
      `the longest prison term for that offence in months` IS A NUMBER @desc The maximum term of imprisonment, in months, for the offence your conduct was (or would have been); 0 if it carries no imprisonment
      `my conduct contributed materially` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your conduct contributed materially to your injury or to the risk of it
      `the defendant's conduct was an offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant's conduct that caused your injury was itself an offence (of any seriousness)
      `the defendant's conduct would have been an offence but for their impairment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant's conduct would have been an offence but for a mental health impairment or cognitive impairment the defendant had at the time
GIVETH A `What my own offence means for my claim`
`What does my own offence mean for my claim` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 3B answer for` `my kind of claim` `and` `Part 7: self-defence and recovery by criminals`
    WHEN JUST g THEN
        BRANCH IF (NOT g) THEN `sections 54 and 54A do not limit my damages`
               IF (NOT `I was hurt during or after my own conduct`) THEN `sections 54 and 54A do not limit my damages`
               IF (NOT `my conduct contributed materially`) THEN `sections 54 and 54A do not limit my damages`
               IF `the longest prison term for that offence in months` LESS THAN 6 THEN `sections 54 and 54A do not limit my damages`
               IF `my conduct` EQUALS `an offence` AND (NOT `the defendant's conduct was an offence`) THEN `no damages: section 54 bars my claim`
               IF     `my conduct` EQUALS `not an offence only because of my mental health impairment or cognitive impairment at the time`
                  AND (NOT `the defendant's conduct was an offence`)
                  AND (NOT `the defendant's conduct would have been an offence but for their impairment`)
               THEN `no damages for non-economic loss or for lost earnings (section 54A)`
               OTHERWISE `sections 54 and 54A do not limit my damages`
    OTHERWISE `the Act does not settle the offence question` -- NOTHING-kind (fork F1)
